1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.570
In the previous lesson, you saw how controlled generative UI

2
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.473
gives you full control and customizability.

3
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.477
But it requires a dedicated component for every interaction.

4
00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:15.682
In this lesson, you'll take a different approach called declarative generative UI.

5
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:19.052
We will use A2UI, which is an open spec

6
00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.854
for declarative generative UI

7
00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:24.591
led by Google in which CopilotKit has deeply collaborated on.

8
00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:27.494
Instead of building a component for every single use case,

9
00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:30.497
you'll define a catalog of Lego-like building blocks

10
00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:33.199
and let the agent assemble those on demand.

11
00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.035
This is an exciting part of the

12
00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:36.870
generative UI spectrum. So let's dive in.

13
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.373
In the previous lesson, we defined a dedicated React component

14
00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:44.077
for each item that we wanted our agents to support.

15
00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:48.181
The benefit was great control over each and every item shown.

16
00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.985
The problem is that every new capability requires a new dedicated component

17
00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:54.788
built from scratch, which is fine at 20 components,

18
00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.390
but quickly becomes painful at 200 components.

19
00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.859
Declarative Generative UI flips this equation.

20
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.662
Rather than defining each component individually,

21
00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.864
we define an upfront catalog which we then

22
00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.267
let the agent assemble together on its own.

23
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.169
Let's dive into what this means specifically.

24
00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:12.472
First, as we mentioned, developers declare a catalog of components

25
00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:15.475
which should be thought of as Lego-like building blocks.

26
00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:17.577
The catalog is defined in two parts.

27
00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.680
First, there are definitions for each atom in the catalog.

28
00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:25.485
which include the item's name, description, and its required props.

29
00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:28.721
And separately, there are renderers defined for each item,

30
00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:31.691
which take a JSON payload describing each component

31
00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:35.195
and return an actual rendered component for the relevant platform.

32
00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.530
In this lesson, we will use React, but the same

33
00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:40.834
pattern extends elsewhere. When a user asks some question,

34
00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:43.536
the agent may decide to answer that question

35
00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.539
by assembling together a declarative generative UI component.

36
00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.675
The agent first emits a Schema,

37
00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.944
which is a structural assembly of items

38
00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:53.947
from the Components Catalog, which is not yet populated with data.

39
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.983
Then the agent emits the data bindings

40
00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.652
that populate that schema with specific values.

41
00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.454
Finally, the schema and the data bindings

42
00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:02.956
are passed on to the front end

43
00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:04.657
where they are served as inputs to a renderer,

44
00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.760
which returns the fully assembled component for the native platform.

45
00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.963
Again, in this case, we will be using React.

46
00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:12.966
The end result is a component which can vary considerably,

47
00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:15.668
but within the confines of a bounded menu that you control.

48
00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.371
Declarative Generative UI has a number of distinct advantages.

49
00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.974
First, it offers flexibility, but with guardrails.

50
00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.676
Your agent can render custom components for a job,

51
00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.379
but will draw from a fixed menu of pre-created components,

52
00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.947
which can be made for example

53
00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.749
both to conform to your design guidelines.

54
00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:31.851
Additionally, because declarative generative UI

55
00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:34.554
is rooted in a structured schema, typically JSON,

56
00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.956
it is suitable for any front-end environment,

57
00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:39.459
not just for web, but also for mobile, Slack,

58
00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:42.862
and other environments. At the same time, declarative generative UI

59
00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.365
is less predictable and customizable

60
00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:47.066
than fully controlled generative UI.

61
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.369
This unique profile of strengths and weaknesses

62
00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:51.070
makes declarative generative UI

63
00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.673
most suited for the long tail of product surfaces

64
00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.275
in consumer-facing applications.

65
00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.878
where flexibility is generally more important

66
00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:01.681
than pixel-perfect designs and predictability.

67
00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:04.184
It's also suitable for internal applications

68
00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:08.288
where functionality and simplicity of implementation often take precedence

69
00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.890
over a optimized experience.

70
00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.892
For example, if you're an airline,

71
00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:14.894
you will likely want your flight card

72
00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:17.697
to be implemented with Controlled Generative UI

73
00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:20.500
for maximum predictability as well as control

74
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.503
of your application's most used surface by far.

75
00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:26.639
If you think about features like tracking a lost laptop

76
00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.042
or getting refunded for a trip, it's more important

77
00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:31.945
that the agent can talk and interact with those abstractions at all

78
00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:35.248
than that it offers a pixel perfect experience.

79
00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:38.251
And so these surfaces are great matches

80
00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.787
for declarative generative UI.

81
00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:44.991
In this course, we will implement declarative generative UI using A2UI.

82
00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:47.894
A2UI is an open, declarative generative UI spec that is

83
00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:51.097
led by Google and which CopilotKit has closely collaborated on.

84
00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:54.701
A2UI implements the declarative generative UI approach we outlined earlier

85
00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:57.103
with a component catalog, a schema,

86
00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.205
data bindings and renderers

87
00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:02.041
using A2UI operations.

88
00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:05.044
A2UI middleware is fully integrated with A2UI,

89
00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:07.747
which means you can bring A2UI declarative generative UI

90
00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.750
to any agent in the stack.

91
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.555
Declarative generative UI comes in two flavors, dynamic and fixed schema.

92
00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:19.259
With the dynamic schema variant, the agent is responsible both

93
00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:21.761
for assembling the schema on the fly

94
00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:24.764
and for populating that schema with data bindings.

95
00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:27.367
With a Fixed Schema variant, you as the

96
00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:29.902
programmer are responsible for defining the schema in advance,

97
00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.904
and the agent is only responsible

98
00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.007
for populating that schema with data bindings.

99
00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:35.608
Now let's jump into some code and

100
00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:36.909
take a look at how this works.

101
00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:40.079
First, just like in the previous modules,

102
00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:42.782
we're going to copy the reset session cell

103
00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.584
and run it to make sure

104
00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:46.686
we're starting from a fresh starting point.

105
00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:49.455
Next, just like before, we're going to set up our dependencies,

106
00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:51.758
starting by installing the Python

